### =====================================================================
### Smooth end-to-end flow (REST Client extension)
###
### Onboards a retailer and a supplier, gets a product offer approved,
### opens a pool, has the retailer join it, pushes the pool to target,
### assigns a delivery, and confirms both parties actually got notified.
### Run every request top-to-bottom with "Send Request" — each step
### captures the ids/tokens the next one needs, so nothing below needs
### hand-editing except the two admin credentials.
###
### Prerequisites:
###   1. Mongo + API running: `docker-compose up -d mongo` and `npm run dev`.
###   2. An admin account — there is no public admin-registration endpoint.
###      The credentials below already point at a throwaway admin seeded
###      into this project's local dev DB (smoothflow-admin@test.local).
###      If you ever wipe that DB, recreate it with:
###        SEED_ADMIN_1_EMAIL=smoothflow-admin@test.local \
###        SEED_ADMIN_1_PASSWORD=SmoothFlowAdmin123 npm run seed:admins
###      or swap in your own admin's email/password below.
###
### Re-running from the top will fail at step 3/4 with 409 Conflict
### (email already registered) — either bump the two emails below or
### skip straight to step 6 (admin login) and reuse the ids/tokens you
### already have from the first run.
### =====================================================================

@root = http://localhost:8000/api/v1
@adminEmail = smoothflow-admin@test.local
@adminPassword = SmoothFlowAdmin123

### 1. Address for the retailer (anonymous — collected before the account exists)

# @name retailerAddress
POST {{root}}/addresses
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "location": "https://maps.app.goo.gl/retailer-warehouse",
  "region": "Muscat",
  "city": "Al Khuwair"
}

###

@retailerAddressId = {{retailerAddress.response.body._id}}

### 2. Address for the soon-to-be supplier

# @name supplierAddress
POST {{root}}/addresses
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "location": "https://maps.app.goo.gl/supplier-warehouse",
  "region": "Al-Batinah",
  "city": "Sohar"
}

###

@supplierAddressId = {{supplierAddress.response.body._id}}

### 3. Register the retailer

# @name registerRetailer
POST {{root}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "firstName": "Rashid",
  "lastName": "Retailer",
  "email": "rashid.retailer@example.com",
  "phoneNumber": "+96891149027",
  "companyName": "Rashid Retail LLC",
  "password": "RetailerPass123",
  "addresses": ["{{retailerAddressId}}"]
}

###

@retailerToken = {{registerRetailer.response.body.accessToken}}

### 4. Register the future supplier (every registration starts as RETAILER only)

# @name registerSupplier
POST {{root}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "firstName": "Salim",
  "lastName": "Supplier",
  "email": "salim.supplier@example.com",
  "phoneNumber": "+96891149028",
  "companyName": "Salim Wholesale Foods",
  "password": "SupplierPass123",
  "addresses": ["{{supplierAddressId}}"]
}

###

@supplierBootstrapToken = {{registerSupplier.response.body.accessToken}}

### 5. That user files a supplier request

# @name supplierRequest
POST {{root}}/supplier-requests
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{supplierBootstrapToken}}

{
  "description": "We are a wholesale rice and cooking-oil distributor looking to sell through OrderPools."
}

###

@supplierRequestId = {{supplierRequest.response.body.data._id}}

### 6. Admin logs in (run `npm run seed:admins` first if this account doesn't exist yet)

# @name adminLogin
POST {{root}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
}

###

@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 7. Admin approves the request — adds SUPPLIER onto the user's existing roles

PATCH {{root}}/supplier-requests/{{supplierRequestId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "APPROVED",
  "adminComment": "Verified commercial registration"
}

### 8. The now-supplier logs in again for a token that actually carries SUPPLIER
###    (roles are baked into the JWT at issue time — the step-4 token still
###    only says RETAILER, and /auth/refresh reuses that same stale payload,
###    so a fresh login is the only way to pick up the new role)

# @name supplierLogin
POST {{root}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "salim.supplier@example.com",
  "password": "SupplierPass123"
}

###

@supplierToken = {{supplierLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 9. Supplier creates a product offer

# @name createOffer
POST {{root}}/offers
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{supplierToken}}

{
  "name": "Basmati Rice 25kg",
  "description": "Long-grain basmati rice, wholesale sack",
  "brand": "Al Reef",
  "unit": "CARTON",
  "wholeQuantity": 500,
  "price": 20
}

###

@offerId = {{createOffer.response.body._id}}

### 10. Admin approves the offer

PATCH {{root}}/offers/{{offerId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "status": "APPROVED",
  "adminComment": "Looks good"
}

### 11. Admin opens a pool for the approved offer.
###     Pool.currentQuantity is the quantity still available to be claimed —
###     it starts here at the amount the pool needs to fill and counts down
###     as participants join, so it's set to 10 (== minimumContribution) to
###     let the single retailer below claim the whole pool in one join.

# @name createPool
POST {{root}}/pools
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "productoffer_ref": "{{offerId}}",
  "currentQuantity": 10,
  "minimumContribution": 10,
  "pricePerUnit": 5,
  "endDate": "2026-12-31T23:59:00Z"
}

###

@poolId = {{createPool.response.body._id}}

### 12. Retailer fetches their own profile to get their user id (needed as Payment.user_ref)

# @name retailerMe
GET {{root}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{retailerToken}}

###

@retailerUserId = {{retailerMe.response.body.user._id}}

### 13. Retailer pays (the payments route has no auth/business-logic wired up yet)

# @name createPayment
POST {{root}}/payments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "user_ref": "{{retailerUserId}}",
  "transactionReference": "txn_smooth_flow_001",
  "amount": 100,
  "stripePaymentIntentId": "pi_smooth_flow_001"
}

###

@paymentId = {{createPayment.response.body._id}}

### 14. Retailer joins the pool, claiming the whole remaining quantity (10).
###     PoolParticipantController.create atomically decrements
###     Pool.currentQuantity by the joined quantity and, since that leaves
###     exactly 0, flips Pool.status straight to TARGET_REACHED — no manual
###     admin step needed for either.

# @name joinPool
POST {{root}}/participants
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{retailerToken}}

{
  "pool_ref": "{{poolId}}",
  "payment_ref": "{{paymentId}}",
  "address_ref": "{{retailerAddressId}}",
  "quantity": 10
}

### 15. Admin assigns the delivery — raises DELIVERY_ASSIGNED, which notifies
###     the offer's supplier and every participant of this pool

# @name createDelivery
POST {{root}}/deliveries
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "pool_ref": "{{poolId}}"
}

###

@deliveryId = {{createDelivery.response.body._id}}

### 16. Confirm the supplier got notified ("Delivery assigned")

GET {{root}}/notifications
Authorization: Bearer {{supplierToken}}

### 17. Confirm the retailer got notified ("Your delivery is on its way")

GET {{root}}/notifications
Authorization: Bearer {{retailerToken}}
